{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_d57yfxg5vp4v36guviqbgob5el44ldifzmc5gvqq52jxzizjqvaa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **The Rust translation is correct — no divergences found.**\n\n## Result\n\nThe library is a single function, `hex2bin`, and the Rust translation matches the C byte-for-byte across every configuration and error path tested.\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` parity | ✅ symbol diff **empty**; 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols |\n| Phase B — all 30 `CONFIGS.md` rows | ✅ pass, randomized (fixed seed) |\n| Binary stdout comparison | N/A — no `add_executable`, no `[[bin]]` |\n| Phase C — all 16 `ERRORS.md` rows | ✅ pass, same *specific* sentinel |\n| All feature combinations | ✅ Cargo.toml has no `[features]`; verified in debug **and** release |\n\n## Artifacts created\n\n- `translation/SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (16 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (30 rows)\n- `translation/tests/harness.rs` — loads **both** `.so`s via `libloading`, never calls Rust directly\n- `translation/tests/configs.rs` (30 tests), `errors.rs` (23), `stress.rs` (4) — **57 tests, all passing**\n- `translation/run-tests.sh` — builds both libs, diffs symbols, loops profiles/feature combos\n\n## Two real issues found and fixed (in the test infrastructure, not the C)\n\n1. **`cargo test` silently tested a stale artifact.** Because the lib is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` only, an integration test can't link it, so cargo never rebuilt it — tests were loading an old `target/release` `.so`. Added an mtime staleness guard that panics, plus `run-tests.sh` which builds first. Without this the whole suite could have passed vacuously.\n2. **Stale doc references** to a `tests/differential.rs` that never existed.\n\n## Harness validated against 6 injected mutations\n\nA green suite is worthless if it can't fail, so I mutated `src/lib.rs` one change at a time:\n\n| mutation | caught |\n|---|---|\n| drop `hex_pos--` rewind | ✅ 10 tests |\n| break the `strchr` NUL quirk | ✅ 3 tests |\n| drop `state == 0` ignore guard | ✅ 2 tests |\n| `c_alpha0` mask off-by-one | ✅ 6 tests |\n| `>=` → `>` on `bin_maxlen` | ✅ SIGSEGV |\n| remove `bin_pos = 0` | ❌ — **and correctly so**: that line is dead in the C too, since `ret != 0` returns `ret` before `bin_pos` is ever read. Not a coverage gap. |\n\n`src/lib.rs` was restored afterward and SHA-256-verified identical to the pristine copy. `c_src/` sources are untouched (only `c_src/build/` was added, as instructed).\n\nNotable C quirks I confirmed the Rust replicates rather than \"fixes\": `strchr(ignore, 0)` matches the ignore set's own terminator, so an embedded NUL is *always* skippable when `ignore != NULL`; ignore chars are suppressed mid-byte (`state != 0`); and the odd-digit path rewinds `hex_pos` so `*hex_end_p` points *back at* the unpaired digit."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":530,"cache_read_input_tokens":108360,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":530,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60bcaed9-b188-42da-be24-d717728c7957","uuid":"119473fd-0fb8-42cc-a8b8-476df612ac2d","timestamp":"2026-09-05T00:55:27.599Z"}
